## Board layer: `boards/` submodule

The hardware types, the module inventory (COM ports) and the pin/capability macros come from the shared repo **`TomHildebrand/esp32-boards`**, a git submodule at `boards/`.

- **Read [boards/CLAUDE.md](boards/CLAUDE.md)** for the module table, COM ports and hardware gotchas.
- Don't edit files under `boards/` here. Make the change in the esp32-boards repo, push it, then run `git submodule update --remote boards` here and commit the new pointer.
- After cloning: `git submodule update --init`.

What belongs to this app:

| Path | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `platformio.ini` | `extra_configs = boards/boards.ini`, `[boards] dir = boards`, `default_envs`, `[env]` with `framework`, app `build_flags`, `lib_deps`; `build_dir` outside OneDrive |
| `src/app_pins.h` | Wiring of external parts per hardware type. Includes `board.h`. Unknown board → `#error`. |
| `src/main.cpp` | The application |
| <`data/`> | <SPIFFS/LittleFS files, uploaded with `-t uploadfs`> |

- Use the `board.h` and `app_pins.h` macros, never hard-coded GPIO numbers.
- Supported hardware: <which hardware types fit, and why others don't (PCB socket, pins, PSRAM, flash)>.

## Commands

```sh
PIO=~/.platformio/penv/Scripts/pio.exe
$PIO run -e m<NN>_...                # build
$PIO run -e m<NN>_... -t upload      # flash firmware
$PIO run -e m<NN>_... -t uploadfs    # flash the filesystem (if data/ exists)
$PIO device monitor -p COM<x> -b 115200
```

- Run `upload` and `uploadfs` as **separate** pio runs. Combined, only the filesystem got written.
- Build output goes to `C:/pio-build/<project>`, outside OneDrive.
